## Queue format

One file per item: `queue/NNNN-slug.md`, with this header, then the draft:

```
---
status: pending        # pending | approved | edited | vetoed | sent
kind: email            # email | post | plan | dm | ask | other
to: <who>
room: <engineers | researchers | funders | families | press | public | ...>
why: <one line: the move, and what it buys>
prediction: <ledger id>
created: <YYYY-MM-DD>
edited_by: <{owner_id} | {slug} | helper>   # set by `queue edit`; only {owner}'s edits are lessons
---
```

Use `gremlin queue` (or the TUI, `gremlin tui`) to approve, edit, veto,
and mark sent. It keeps the first original below `<!-- original -->` and
stamps dates; `gremlin lessons` reads {owner}'s edits and vetoes back.
Bets: `gremlin ledger add`, `due`, `settle`, `score`.

## The corpus, and the wall

When a job sends you to {owner}'s transcripts, read only `corpus/`: the
corpus with the held-out conversations removed. `data/` and `eval/` are
denied in this home's Claude settings, and so is the eval store outside
it: a wall, not a sign. The eval is how {owner} finds out whether you're
any good; reading it would make the answer meaningless.

## Work context, and the other wall

Who {owner} is (persona/) is not what {owner} works on. Work context is
the repos and docs dirs registered with `gremlin context add`; the list
lives in `context/contexts.json` in your home, and `gremlin context list`
prints it. Those paths are where the context is. Read it there, when the
work needs it.

Context never goes into `persona/`, `STATUS.md` or `ideas.md`: not quoted,
not summarized, not "just the names." Those are the files a plate is made
of, and a plate is the thing {owner} might share. If something you learned
at work looks like it belongs in the persona, it doesn't; tell {owner} and
let them decide in their own words. If `gremlin wake` says WORK HOME,
nothing in this home becomes a plate, and meatbag won't run here.
